    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d26">

                <pb facs="0033" n="19" sameAs="frus:frus1942v06/pg19"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">825.857/26: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Chile</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Bowers</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Santiago</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-04-06T18:00:00-04:00">April 6, 1942—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 9:26 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>528. For Acting Secretary. My telegram No. 464, March 23, 8 p.m.<anchor n="30"
                        xml:id="d26-f1"/> Saw Barros Jarpa<anchor n="31" xml:id="d26-f2"/> this
                    morning and was told that the Japanese Minister struck out the reference to the
                    Panama Canal, and that he, Barros Jarpa, has called in the Japanese Minister to
                    say that Chile would look upon an attack on the Canal as an attack on Chile
                    since the economic life of Chile is dependent upon the Canal. This is also what
                    Rossetti told me he had said to the Minister. Barros Jarpa showed me the
                    original Japanese note pledging Japan not to interfere with Chile’s commerce
                    with the United States. This probably means Chilean commerce conveyed in Chilean
                    ships. It probably means that submarine attacks on our own vessels may possibly
                    be made but not on Chilean, but Japan now knows that an attack on the Canal
                    would be considered by Chile an attack on herself.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Panama.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bowers</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d26-f1" n="30" xml:id="d26-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d26-f2" n="31" xml:id="d26-f2-content">
                <p>Chilean Minister for Foreign Affairs; he succeeded Rossetti in this office after
                    the inauguration of President Juan Antonio Ríos on April 2, 1942.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
